><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250630111712+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="d6924da3-8347-4822-8c09-7557b13f6d8d" date="D:20250630111712+10'00'" name="1d75e9cf-374a-4b27-ba78-1d2a7d3f3e5e" icon="Comment" page="5" rect="312.656799,568.213257,336.656799,592.213257" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The day of the menstrual cycle had minimal relationships with&#xD;sleep characteristics, with the exception of sleep duration, in elite&#xD;female soccer players who had regular-length menstrual cycles&#xD;and were identified as good sleepers. Rather, the total symptoms&#xD;reported had a relationship with sleep characteristics demonstrated&#xD;by increased sleep duration and wake after sleep onset. Total&#xD;menstrual cycle symptoms were highest on day 1 of the menstrual&#xD;cycle, before decreasing in the subsequent days. Monitoring&#xD;menstrual cycle symptoms should be encouraged when working&#xD;with female athletes due to potential relationships with sleep&#xD;characteristics.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="612.000000,500.213257,796.000000,592.213257"
/></text
></annots
